{"id": "section-body-25230", "title": "Renal Cell Carcinoma", "chapterId": "chapter-twenty-two", "tocLevel": 4, "tocPath": ["APPROACH TO PATIENTS WITH EMERGENCY CONDITIONS", "MALIGNANT DISEASE CONDITIONS", "Genitourinary Malignancies", "Renal Cell Carcinoma"], "blocks": [{"id": "body-25230", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Renal Cell Carcinoma", "runs": [{"text": "Renal"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Cell"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Carcinoma"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 2, "numberingId": "410"}}, {"id": "body-25231", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Renal cell carcinoma (RCC) constitutes 80–95% of primary renal neoplasms. There are four subtypes of RCC: clear cell (75– 85%), chromophilic/papillary (10–15%), chromophobic (5–10%), oncocytic (3–5%), and collecting duct (very rare). RCC is a radioresistant tumor and has low response rates to cytotoxic chemotherapies, though targeted molecular agents and stereotactic ablative radiotherapy are redefining the management of the disease.", "runs": [{"text": "Renal cell carcinoma (RCC) constitutes 80–95% of primary renal neoplasms. There are four subtypes of RCC: clear cell (75– 85%), chromophilic/papillary (10–15%), chromophobic (5–10%), oncocytic (3–5%), and collecting duct (very rare). RCC is a radioresistant tumor and has low response rates to cytotoxic chemotherapies, though targeted molecular agents and stereotactic ablative radiotherapy are redefining the management of the disease."}]}, {"id": "body-25233", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Risk factors", "runs": [{"text": "Risk"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "factors"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-25234", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Risk factors: tobacco, occupational exposure to cadmium/asbestos/petrols, obesity, acquired cystic disease of the kidney, and phenacetin-containing analgesics exposure.", "runs": [{"text": "Risk"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "factors:"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "tobacco,"}